    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm reviewing a commercial lease for a retail tenant in Singapore. Can you explain the standard legal implications of a \"force majeure\" clause in the event of a prolonged, unforeseen business interruption that prevents the tenant from operating?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under Singapore law, a force majeure event that grounds you to pay rent is strictly limited to \"said Act of God\" where the damage or loss is so great and so sudden that the simple skill and judgment of a reasonable man cannot prevent it. This means that even with a prolonged business interruption, you generally remain liable to pay rent unless the contract specifically lists that specific type of disruption as a qualifying force majeure event for rent relief. Additionally, the relief usually applies only to the extent that the landlord cannot use the premises, so temporary closures or market shifts often do not automatically extend lease terms or waive payment obligations.",
      "source": "target",
