    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you look into what happens if the clause is vaguely worded? like if it just says \"unforeseen circumstances beyond the control of the parties\" but doesn't give specific examples. how would a court typically interpret that kind of ambiguity when there's a dispute?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Singapore courts typically interpret vague or broadly worded force majeure clauses using the objective test, focusing on whether a \"reasonable person\" in the position of the parties could have foreseen the specific circumstances without the passage of time. Consequently, a court is unlikely to extend \"unforeseen circumstances\" to include slow-moving events like economic downturns but may cover unpredictable sudden events like pandemics or extreme weather if they genuinely exceed normal commercial risk. If the clause remains ambiguous after this analysis, courts will often strive to give words their natural and plain meaning to uphold the commercial purpose of the lease rather than rewriting it based on the result of the dispute.",
      "source": "target",
